## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end reliability of streaming reconciliation systems, focusing on critical aspects like idempotency, exactly-once semantics, drift detection, and rollback mechanisms under high throughput conditions—essential for fintech and large-scale payment systems.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end testing in streaming pipelines
- Idempotent reconciliation and exactly-once semantics
- Drift detection and rollback/replay strategies
- Test data generation for duplicates and out-of-order events
- Performance validation at scale (1M events/sec)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: synthetic event generator with duplicates
function genEvents(n){
  const events=[];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    events.push({id: i, timestamp: Date.now(), amount: Math.random()*1000});
    if(Math.random()<0.1) events.push(events[events.length-1]); // 10% duplicates
  }
  return events.sort(() => Math.random()-0.5); // Shuffle for out-of-order
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for streaming pipelines must cover correctness under huge load, fault tolerance, and operational safety. This question probes end-to-end strategy, data quality, drift, and rollback semantics, not isolated unit tests.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming test harness
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics
- Latency budgets and tail latency
- Backpressure and circuit breakers
- Data quality and drift detection
- Rollback and reconciliation
- Replayable synthetic data generation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test snippet showing a Kafka consumer with sequence checks
const consumer = new KafkaConsumer({ groupId: 'tester' });
let lastSeq = 0;
consumer.on('message', (msg) => {
  if (msg.seq <= lastSeq) throw new Error('out-of-order');
  lastSeq = msg.seq;
  // validate idempotent write
  writeIfNotExists(msg.key, msg.value);
});
```

